	<tr><td style="padding: 1px 1px 0 1px;">
		<table width="100%" cellpadding="3" cellspacing="0" border="0">
			<tr><td class="windowbg">
				<table width="100%" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="table-layout: fixed;">
					<tr>
						<td valign="top" width="16%" rowspan="2" style="overflow: hidden;">
							<b><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?action=profile;u=1645" title="View the profile of highschoolteacher">highschoolteacher</a></b>
							<div class="smalltext">
								Newbie<br />
								<img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/star.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><br />
								Posts: 4<br />
								<br />
								
								
								
								<br />
								<a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?action=profile;u=1645"><img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/icons/profile_sm.gif" alt="View Profile" title="View Profile" border="0" /></a>
							</div>
						</td>
						<td valign="top" width="85%" height="100%">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=2209.msg27409#msg27409"><img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=2209.msg27409#msg27409">(Rich) White People and Blue Scholars</a></b>
									<div class="smalltext">&#171; <b> on:</b> May 16, 2007, 10:23:19 am &#187;</div></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;">A long time ago I went to a Dead Prez concert at the showbox (the one with the roots and slum village, and all of them).&nbsp; I had long been a fan of Dead Prez for their critique of American society, racism, hip hop, etc. While the show was real hype, a funny thing happened. As Dead Prez rapped about using violence to dismantle the white, male power structure that dominates American society, hundreds of white men in the audience rapped along with them and even put up a fist of resistance. Later, the predominately white audience loudly sang along to the song &quot;I&#039;m an African.&quot;<br /><br />Today, the Blue Scholars create music that not only questions dominant systems of power in our society but incites their listeners to actually do dismantle these structures. Yet, the Blue Scholars play sold out shows for predominately white crowds. Crowds from my experience that are primarily rich or middle class. Additionally, I would bet that as most of the Blue Scholars album sales are local, their primary consumers (which I would not mistake for constituency) are rich white folks.<br /><br />Now some of this is purely a result of being a hip hop artist in Seattle. The city is overwhelmingly white and becoming more so daily. Additionally, the city tends to be more wealthy with the highest per capita college graduate rate in the country.<br /><br />Yet, I still can&#039;t help but get frustrated when I see a crowd of middle and upper-class white folks singing about destroying the systems of privilege which they have gained so much from. I&#039;m not saying that people shouldn&#039;t feel free to listen and enjoy the music they like.<br /><br />&nbsp;But where is the critical engagement with the music? Where is the critical engagement with the message? How can we have a crowd full of white people singing about the destruction of capitalism, imperialism, and white supremacy, when those same people will become complacent and even revel in the privilege of such systems the very next day? How can we have some people that are so white and so rich be so alienated from the music that they consume?<br /><br />&nbsp;Or for my scholars of Marx, what is it about the context of Seattle and the backpacker hip-hop movement of the U.S that such an explicitly political message like that of the Blue Scholars can become commodified and fetishized to the point that fans that memorize the words yet refuse to internalize the message?<br /><br />I&#039;m really not saying all of this to question the Blue Scholars or the music that they make. Because I really do like them. But I do question the motivations of the so called underground hip hop movement.<br /><br />One author has gone as far as claiming that such tendencies really reflect the racist underpinnings that are central to this movement. (see below)<br /><br />What are other people&#039;s thoughts?<br /><br />Aricle: <a href="http&#58;//democracyandhiphop.blogspot.com/2006/04/notes-on-hip-hop-conservatism.html#links" target="_blank">http://democracyandhiphop.blogspot.com/2006/04/notes-on-hip-hop-conservatism.html#links</a><br /><br /></div>
						</td>
					</tr>
