							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;"><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=2209.msg27409#msg27409">Quote from: highschoolteacher on May 16, 2007, 10:23:19 am</a></div><div class="quote">But where is the critical engagement with the music? Where is the critical engagement with the message? How can we have a crowd full of white people singing about the destruction of capitalism, imperialism, and white supremacy, when those same people will become complacent and even revel in the privilege of such systems the very next day? How can we have some people that are so white and so rich be so alienated from the music that they consume?<br /></div><br />As someone who is &quot;middle class white folk&quot; I honestly don&#039;t think that my opinions or stance about flaws in the system or racism are lessened due to the fact that I grew up in the suburbs or that I happen to be white.  <br /><br />I don&#039;t think the focus should be on who is in the audience, but that all kinds of people are coming together to acknowledge what is messed up in the world today.  <br /><br /></div>
