								Sr. Member<br />
								<img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/staradmin.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/staradmin.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/staradmin.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/staradmin.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/staradmin.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><br />
								Posts: 433<br />
								<br />
								
								
								
								<br />
								<a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?action=profile;u=3"><img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/icons/profile_sm.gif" alt="View Profile" title="View Profile" border="0" /></a>
							</div>
						</td>
						<td valign="top" width="85%" height="100%">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=2209.msg27435#msg27435"><img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=2209.msg27435#msg27435">Re: (Rich) White People and Blue Scholars</a></b>
									<div class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #12 on:</b> May 16, 2007, 12:21:09 pm &#187;</div></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;"><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=2209.msg27422#msg27422">Quote from: highschoolteacher on May 16, 2007, 11:13:18 am</a></div><div class="quote">First, maharlika&#039;s first comment: most definitely. Again, I don&#039;t really question the motivations of the artists that are forced to negotiate their consumers and their constituency.</div><br />aside from sounding very over-intellectualized, you&#039;re critique and questions are interesting. It would be nice to know your background a bit - do you teach in the public school system, what subject, etc.<br /><br /><div class="quoteheader">Quote</div><div class="quote">Second. Antigirl: The point is not that we are talking about how the world is messed up. The point is that the message of the music is asking us to evaluate (critically examine) our position maintaining the messed up world and then do something about it. When does this self evaluation process occur?</div><br />that is up to the individual. Every person in our world has a different role to play. As entertainers, Blue Scholars are using their position on stage to educate and entertain. As individuals they do a ton of work in the community. As a father, Geo&#039;s son is a huge priority for him, Hopefully all of the work they do influences people in different ways over the long run. But historically, you can look at major shifts in people&#039;s view of the world and they correspond to art that does the same. I&#039;d argue that changes in art often come first and drive the changes in mass thought. That&#039;s not an immediate process though, but one step is definitely groups like Blue Scholars feeding the process.<br /><br />as for the article below, it has one huge underlying flaw that from the beginning invalidates it for me altogether - the assumption that there is a &#039;true school&#039; movement, and that Blue Scholars are part of it. That is just a huge generalization. Some other flaws...<br /><br /><div class="quoteheader">Quote</div><div class="quote">Murs’ medium of circulation is mostly white students of liberal arts and children of professionals, while Keak’s is almost unanimously poor, black, unemployed or working-class. This is the irony of many hip-hop conservatives considering their Lefty politics. Another ironic factor is that both artists are underground; neither is contracted to large music monopolies. It is their social base which is different.</div><br />maybe this was written a while ago, but Murs is on Warner&#039;s. As for their audience, unless this writer can back up his assumptions here his argument is based on sweeping generalizations (again) with no specific data to support them. I would actually argue that most of the fan base of massline fans are working class or children of working class and middle class families. Though, I don&#039;t see that as being very relevant and I have no way of proving it without serious polling. <br /><br />Anyhow, I always find it funny that people like highschool teacher find time to question artists with an actual point of view, artists who are active in serious community and political work, whose lyrics come out of that, to artists who have either nothing important or negative things to say and who do little community or political work. The artists with something to say get critiqued, their fans get critiqued, and god forbid those fans cross over to a broader audience than just the group&#039;s core fanbase. Maybe the question should be about how entertainers can use their platform to better promote progressive ideas, and how do we respond to the artists who do nothing or actually do things that promote negative social concepts? Either way, artists put themselves out their so any questioning if fair I guess.<br /><br />Anyhow, good discussion, thanks to highschoolteacher for bringing it up. </div>
						</td>
					</tr>
