								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=2209.msg27441#msg27441"><img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=2209.msg27441#msg27441">Re: (Rich) White People and Blue Scholars</a></b>
									<div class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #14 on:</b> May 16, 2007, 12:39:44 pm &#187;</div></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;"><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=2209.msg27435#msg27435">Quote from: Fuzed on May 16, 2007, 12:21:09 pm</a></div><div class="quote">as for the article below, it has one huge underlying flaw that from the beginning invalidates it for me altogether - the assumption that there is a &#039;true school&#039; movement, and that Blue Scholars are part of it. That is just a huge generalization. <br /></div>the article doesn&#039;t mention Blue Scholars. that&#039;s highschool&#039;s take. <br />also i don&#039;t see the existence of a &#039;true school&#039; movement as being in question.<br /><br /><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=2209.msg27435#msg27435">Quote from: Fuzed on May 16, 2007, 12:21:09 pm</a></div><div class="quote">Anyhow, I always find it funny that people like highschool teacher find time to question artists with an actual point of view, artists who are active in serious community and political work, whose lyrics come out of that, to artists who have either nothing important or negative things to say and who do little community or political work. The artists with something to say get critiqued, their fans get critiqued, and god forbid those fans cross over to a broader audience than just the group&#039;s core fanbase. </div>de-fen-sive!<br /><br /><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=2209.msg27435#msg27435">Quote from: Fuzed on May 16, 2007, 12:21:09 pm</a></div><div class="quote">Maybe the question should be about how entertainers can use their platform to better promote progressive ideas, and how do we respond to the artists who do nothing or actually do things that promote negative social concepts? </div>there&#039;s plenty of discourse about artists promoting bullshit right now. it&#039;s at a fever pitch!<br /><br /><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=2209.msg27435#msg27435">Quote from: Fuzed on May 16, 2007, 12:21:09 pm</a></div><div class="quote">Either way, artists put themselves out their so any questioning if fair I guess.<br /></div>well exactly. i think it&#039;s bullshit that just because shit is &#039;positive&#039; it gets a pass and is exempt from critical thought. such an approach cheapens the art.</div>
						</td>
					</tr>
